    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          A portable dryer is a trade: you give up capacity and speed in exchange for a machine that runs on a standard 120V outlet and fits in a closet. That trade is worth making in plenty of apartments, but only if you respect two things a small dryer still shares with a big one. The hot, wet, lint-filled air has to go somewhere safe, and the heater draws enough current to deserve its own circuit.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-matters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Actually Matters in a Portable Dryer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start with the electrical supply, because it decides which dryers are even on the table. A standard full-size electric dryer runs on 240V and needs a dedicated 30A circuit and a large receptacle. A portable or compact 120V dryer plugs into an ordinary household outlet. If your apartment has no dryer hookup, a 120V machine is usually the only electric dryer you can use without an electrician, which is why this guide focuses on them.
          </p>

          <p>
            Next is venting. A conventional dryer heats air, blows it through the clothes and pushes it out of the machine carrying water vapor and lint. That exhaust has to go outdoors through a proper duct or window vent, or the machine has to be a genuine ventless design that condenses the moisture inside. Where the exhaust goes is the single biggest practical question for anyone putting a dryer in an apartment, and it is covered in detail below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then capacity. Compact dryers run from small tabletop units of around 1.5 cu ft to roughly 3.5 to 4 cu ft for the larger compact models. A full-size dryer is typically well over 7 cu ft. The dryer drum should be roughly twice the size of the washer drum it pairs with, because clothes need room to tumble. A 2.6 cu ft dryer pairs well with a 1.6 cu ft portable washer; a tiny 1.5 cu ft unit is for very small loads.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, speed. A 120V heater is much smaller than a 240V heater, so a compact dryer takes longer to dry the same weight of clothes. That is not a defect; it is physics. How wet the clothes are going in matters a lot, which is why the spin speed of your washer and a separate spin dryer can cut drying time more than any setting on the dryer itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are still choosing the washer, plan the pair together. Our <a href="/best-portable-washer/">guide to portable washers</a> covers the sink-connected machines that pair with these dryers, including twin tubs whose separate spinners leave clothes drier going in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="120v-vs-240v" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. 120V vs 240V: Why a Compact Dryer Is Slower
          </h2>

          <p>
            The difference between a 120V and a 240V dryer is mostly about how much heat the machine can make. Power is voltage times current. A 240V dryer on a 30A circuit can run a heating element several times larger than anything that can safely run on a 120V household circuit. More heat means more water evaporated per minute, which is why a full-size electric dryer finishes a big load in under an hour while a compact 120V model takes longer on a much smaller one.
          </p>

          <p>
            A 120V compact dryer is limited by the outlet. A typical 15A or 20A household circuit can only supply so much power, and a dryer runs for long stretches, so its heater has to be sized well under the circuit's limit. The result is a machine that dries gently and slowly. For many people that is fine, especially for a single person running a load in the evening, but it is worth knowing before you expect full-size performance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some larger compact dryers are built for 240V, often with a choice of 120V or 240V depending on the model. They dry faster, but they need a 240V receptacle and circuit, usually the same 30A dryer circuit a full-size machine would use. If your apartment already has a dryer hookup, a compact 240V model or even a stackable full-depth pair may be a better choice than a portable one. Our <a href="/best-dryer-cord/">dryer cord guide</a> covers the three- and four-prong cords and receptacles those machines use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not try to adapt between the two. A 240V dryer cannot be safely plugged into a 120V outlet with an adapter, and a 120V dryer should never be wired onto a 240V circuit. Buy the dryer that matches the receptacle you have, or have a licensed electrician install the receptacle you need, with a permit where your jurisdiction requires one.
          </p>

