          <p>
            What the table cannot show is drying time, because it depends far more on how wet the clothes are going in than on the dryer. A load spun hard in a fast washer dries much sooner in a compact dryer than the same load out of a small portable washer with a gentle spin. That is why the spin dryer earns a place on this list despite not heating anything.
          </p>

          <p>
            Controls vary from a simple timer knob to electronic panels with sensor drying. Sensor drying, where the machine stops when the clothes are dry, saves energy and reduces over-drying, but the main thing a compact dryer needs is a clear lint filter that is easy to reach and clean. Check where the filter sits before you buy, because one that is fiddly to remove is one you will be tempted to skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are tempted by an all-in-one washer-dryer combo, treat it as a separate category. Combos save space but are usually larger, heavier, more expensive and slower per load than a separate washer and dryer. They make sense where you can only fit one machine. For a renter who moves every couple of years, a separate portable washer and compact dryer is usually the more flexible choice.
