        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Venting, Vent Kits and Ventless Designs
          </h2>

          <p>
            A conventional dryer, including most compact 120V models, is a vented dryer. It pushes hot, moist, lint-laden air out of an exhaust port, and that air has to go outdoors. Residential codes generally require dryer exhaust to terminate outside the building, and dryer manuals specify the duct type and maximum length. Venting a standard dryer into a closet or living room dumps moisture and fine lint into the space, which feeds mold, damages drywall and leaves lint on every surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            For apartments without a wall vent, the usual answer is a window vent kit: a panel that fits in the window opening with a duct port, connected to the dryer by a short duct. Keep the run as short and straight as possible, use rigid or semi-rigid metal duct rather than plastic or thin foil, and make sure the outside end has a flap that closes when the dryer is off. Long, kinked or crushed ducts trap lint and slow drying, and both raise the fire risk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some compact dryer manuals describe an indoor exhaust option, such as a lint bag or filter box on the exhaust port. Only use it if your specific manual permits it, and understand what it does and does not do. It catches some lint, but all of the water still goes into the room. A load of wet clothes holds a surprising amount of water, and in a small apartment that humidity condenses on windows and walls. If you go this route, run a dehumidifier; our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier guide</a> covers models that can keep up.
          </p>

          <p>
            A true ventless dryer is a different design. Condenser dryers cool the exhaust air inside the machine and collect the water in a tank or send it to a drain; heat pump dryers do the same more efficiently. They are built to run without an outdoor vent, but they still release some heat, need their lint filters and condenser cleaned and usually cost more. Most ventless models are full-size or 24-inch compact units, many on 240V. A vented dryer with an indoor lint bag is not a ventless dryer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Never vent any dryer into a wall cavity, attic, crawl space or ceiling, and never connect it to a bathroom fan duct. All of these trap lint and moisture where you cannot see or clean them. If you cannot vent outdoors and your manual does not permit indoor use, either choose a genuine ventless design or air-dry on a rack. That is not the answer people want, but it is the safe one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="circuits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Outlets, Dedicated Circuits and Extension Cords
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 120V compact dryer is one of the largest loads you can plug into an ordinary outlet. Its heater runs for long stretches, which is exactly the kind of load that exposes weak connections and overloaded circuits. Check the nameplate or manual for the dryer's rated current and the circuit it requires. Many manuals call for a dedicated circuit, meaning nothing else is plugged into that circuit while the dryer runs.
          </p>

          <p>
            A dedicated 20A circuit is the conservative choice for a heated compact dryer, and some manuals specify it. A 15A circuit shared with a microwave, a space heater or a hair dryer will trip, and repeated tripping is a sign the circuit is being pushed too hard. If you are not sure what else is on the circuit, our <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder guide</a> covers the tools that map outlets to breakers in minutes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plug the dryer straight into a wall outlet. Do not use an extension cord, a power strip or a light-duty adapter. The plug and outlet should fit snugly; a loose, warm or discolored outlet is a warning sign, and you should stop using it and have it checked. Adding a new circuit or upgrading an outlet is a job for a licensed electrician, with a permit where required, and in a rental it is the landlord's decision and usually the landlord's cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electronic controls on newer dryers are sensitive to voltage spikes from lightning and utility switching. A point-of-use surge protector rated for appliances helps, and a whole-house surge protector at the panel protects every appliance at once. Our <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector guide</a> explains how those devices work and why they need an electrician to install them.
          </p>

          <p>
            A heated dryer is not a good match for most portable power stations. Its heater draws a large continuous load that would drain a typical station quickly. A generator can run one, but only outdoors, far from doors and windows, because generator exhaust contains carbon monoxide. Never run a generator in a garage or indoors, and never plug one into a wall outlet to backfeed the house. During an outage, a spin dryer and a drying rack are the practical choice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="loads-times" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Load Sizes and Drying Times
          </h2>

          <p>
            Compact dryers work best with modest loads. The drum needs empty space for clothes to tumble through the hot air; a packed drum dries slowly, unevenly and with more wrinkles. A good rule is to fill the drum about half to two-thirds full with wet clothes. If you are pairing it with a portable washer, one washer load per dryer load is usually about right, and splitting a big wash across two dryer runs often finishes sooner than one overloaded run.
          </p>

          <p>
            Drying time depends mainly on how much water is in the clothes, the fabric and the heater size. Light synthetics dry quickly; cotton towels and jeans take longest. Manufacturers rarely publish meaningful drying times for compact dryers because the variables are so large. Owners commonly report that a mixed load from a small portable washer takes noticeably longer than an hour, while the same load spun harder dries faster. Use the times on your manual as a starting point, not a promise.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cheapest way to speed up a compact dryer is to put drier clothes into it. Use the highest spin your washer offers, run an extra spin on heavy items, or use a separate spin dryer before the heated dryer. Removing water by spinning takes far less energy than evaporating it with heat, and it shortens the dryer's run time, which also reduces wear and the amount of lint in the exhaust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sort by weight. Drying towels and jeans with T-shirts means the light items finish long before the heavy ones, and the dryer keeps running to finish the towels while the shirts overheat. Separate loads of similar weight dry more evenly and faster. Clean the lint filter before every load, because a clogged filter restricts airflow and adds time to every cycle.
          </p>

