        <div id="stacking-fire" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Stacking Kits, Wall Mounts and Lint Fire Safety
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many compact front-load dryers can be stacked on top of a matching compact front-load washer with a stacking kit sold by the manufacturer, and some can be wall-mounted with a manufacturer kit. Use only the kit specified in the manual for your exact models, because the kit has to hold the dryer's weight and keep it from walking off the washer during spin. Never stack a dryer on top of a top-loading portable washer, which has a lid that needs to open and a cabinet not built to carry weight.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you wall-mount a dryer, mount it into studs or solid blocking with the hardware the manufacturer specifies, at a height where you can reach the lint filter without strain. A dryer pulled off a wall by its own vibration is a serious hazard. If you are not confident in finding studs and choosing fasteners, hire someone who is. In a rental, ask before drilling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lint is the fire risk to take seriously. The NFPA identifies failure to clean the dryer as a leading cause of home dryer fires, and lint is highly flammable. Clean the lint filter before every load. Every few months, disconnect and clean the duct and the vent kit, and check the outdoor flap opens freely. If the dryer is suddenly taking longer, the outside of the machine feels hot, or you smell scorching, stop using it and check the duct.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the dryer when you are home and awake. Do not start a load and leave the apartment or go to sleep. Keep the area around the dryer clear of boxes, laundry baskets and cleaning products, and have a working smoke alarm nearby. Do not dry items stained with oil, gasoline, cooking oil or solvents, which can ignite even after washing; air-dry those instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Moisture is the other long-term issue. Even a properly vented dryer warms and humidifies the room through its cabinet and door seals. In a small, closed space, keep the door open or add ventilation. If you see condensation on windows or musty smells after laundry day, our <a href="/best-air-purifier-for-mold/">guide to air purifiers for mold</a> and dehumidifier guides cover how to bring humidity back under control.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="which-to-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Which Portable Dryer to Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            If you have a standard outlet on a circuit you can dedicate to the dryer and a window you can vent through, buy the Magic Chef MCSDRY15W. Its 2.6 cu ft drum is the right size to pair with a 1.6 cu ft portable washer, it runs on 120V, and Magic Chef's compact laundry line is widely sold and easy to find support for. It is the default pick for most apartments.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already own or want a BLACK+DECKER portable washer, the BLACK+DECKER BCED26 is the matching alternative at a similar size. If you wash bigger loads and have the electrical supply for it, look at the larger BCED37, but confirm the voltage and circuit requirement on the listing first. A larger drum is only worth it if your outlet and circuit can support the machine as specified.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are drying a few items at a time, live alone in a very small space or need the lowest price, a mini dryer like the COSTWAY Compact Laundry Dryer handles small loads of light clothing. Do not expect it to dry towels and jeans for two people. It still needs to be vented properly and plugged directly into the wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you cannot vent a heated dryer, or you want to cut the time and cost of any dryer, buy the Laundry Alternative Nina Soft Spin Dryer. It uses no heat, so it adds no humidity beyond what drips into its drain, and it pulls a lot of water out of a load in a few minutes. Paired with a drying rack, it is a complete drying setup for many apartments, and paired with a heated compact dryer, it shortens every cycle.
          </p>

          <p>
            Skip any compact dryer with a vague listing, no clear model number and no downloadable manual. You need the installation instructions to vent and power the machine correctly, and you need a real brand behind it for parts like belts and heating elements. Also skip the idea of venting a standard dryer into the room to save on a vent kit; the vent kit is cheaper than a mold problem.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Venting a standard dryer into the room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A conventional dryer pushes hot, wet, lint-filled air out of its exhaust. Dumped into a closet or living space, that means mold, damp walls and lint everywhere. Vent outdoors with a short metal duct and window kit, use an indoor exhaust option only if your manual explicitly permits it, or choose a true ventless design.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using an extension cord or shared circuit</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heated dryer is a long, heavy load that overheats light cords and power strips and trips shared circuits. Plug it directly into a wall outlet, check the nameplate current and keep the circuit free of other heavy loads. If the outlet is loose or warm, stop and have a licensed electrician look at it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping lint cleaning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lint is highly flammable, and failure to clean is a leading cause of home dryer fires. Clean the lint filter before every load, clean the duct and vent kit every few months, and treat longer drying times, a hot cabinet or a scorching smell as a reason to stop and check the duct immediately.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading a compact drum</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A packed drum dries slowly and unevenly and runs the heater longer than necessary. Fill the drum about half to two-thirds full, sort loads by weight and split a big wash across two runs. Two moderate loads often finish sooner than one stuffed load, with fewer wrinkles and less wear.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 240V dryer for a 120V outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Full-size and some compact dryers need a 240V receptacle on a dedicated 30A circuit, and no adapter makes them safe on a household outlet. Check the electrical requirement on the spec sheet before buying, and if you need a new receptacle, have a licensed electrician install it with any required permit.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

