        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best portable dryer for an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most apartments, a 120V compact dryer around 2.6 cu ft, such as the Magic Chef MCSDRY15W or BLACK+DECKER BCED26, is the best fit. It plugs into a standard outlet, pairs well with a 1.6 cu ft portable washer and fits in a closet. You will still need a way to vent it outdoors, usually a window vent kit, and ideally a dedicated circuit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a 120V clothes dryer plug into a regular outlet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, that is the point of a 120V compact dryer. Plug it directly into a grounded wall outlet, never into an extension cord or power strip, and keep other heavy loads off that circuit while it runs. Many manuals call for a dedicated circuit, often 20A. Check the nameplate and manual, and have a licensed electrician add a circuit if needed.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it safe to use a portable dryer without a vent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not a standard vented dryer. Without a vent, the moisture and fine lint go into your living space, causing mold and damp walls. Use an indoor exhaust option only if your manual explicitly permits it, and run a dehumidifier if you do. A true ventless condenser or heat pump dryer is designed to run without a vent, or use a spin dryer and a rack.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a portable dryer take to dry a load?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Longer than a full-size dryer, because a 120V heater is much smaller. Drying time depends mostly on how wet the clothes are, the fabric and the load size, so manufacturers rarely publish firm times. Owners commonly report that loads from small portable washers can take well over an hour. Spinning clothes harder before drying is the most effective way to shorten it.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a ventless dryer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A ventless dryer removes moisture from the exhaust inside the machine instead of blowing it outdoors. Condenser dryers cool the air and collect water in a tank or drain line; heat pump dryers do the same more efficiently. They are designed to run without an outdoor vent. A vented dryer with an indoor lint bag is not a ventless dryer, because the moisture still enters the room.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I stack a portable dryer on a portable washer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only with a matching front-load compact washer and the manufacturer's stacking kit for those exact models. Never stack a dryer on a top-loading portable washer, whose lid has to open and whose cabinet is not built to carry weight. Some compact dryers can be wall-mounted with a manufacturer kit, which must be fastened into studs or solid blocking.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I run a portable dryer on a generator or power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A heated dryer would drain most portable power stations quickly because its heater is a large continuous load. A generator can run one, but only outdoors, well away from doors and windows, because of carbon monoxide. Never run a generator indoors or in a garage, and never plug one into a wall outlet. In an outage, a spin dryer and rack are more practical.
              </p>
            </div>

